/// =====================================================================================
/// agg_merchant_daily — grain: one merchant, one day
///
/// Exposed as its own table, **not** as a Power BI user-defined aggregation over
/// fact_transaction. The difference matters and the reason is `distinct_account_count`.
///
/// A user-defined aggregation lets the engine silently substitute this table for the detail fact
/// whenever a query's grain permits. For the additive columns that substitution is a pure win and
/// is always correct. For `distinct_account_count` it is not correct at any grain coarser than a
/// day, and the engine cannot tell the difference — a distinct count is not an aggregation of
/// distinct counts. So the substitution would be right for the seven additive columns and wrong for
/// the eighth, silently, at query time. Making the table explicit means a report author chooses
/// it, and the measures below are where that choice is fenced.
///
/// Two further hazards with an explicitly-exposed aggregate, named here because they are the ones
/// that produce a plausible wrong number rather than an error:
///
///   * **It relates only to `dim_date` and `dim_merchant`.** There is no path to dim_account,
///     dim_currency, dim_decline_reason or the channel and 3DS columns — those live at
///     transaction grain and were aggregated away. A visual that slices an aggregate measure by
///     `fact_transaction[channel]` does not get an error; it gets the unfiltered total repeated
///     in every channel row. Every measure here is therefore in the `Merchant daily (agg)`
///     display folder, kept away from the detail measures it superficially duplicates.
///   * **It is a full rebuild, not a merge**, so it is consistent with fact_transaction at the end
///     of a run and never mid-run. Nothing in this model can express that, which is why
///     `tests/test_gold_recon.py` ties the two by hand after every load.
/// =====================================================================================
table agg_merchant_daily
	lineageTag: d7162496-e970-50ee-afee-727d3b3f82bd

	column date_sk
		dataType: int64
		isHidden
		summarizeBy: none
		sourceColumn: date_sk

	column merchant_sk
		dataType: int64
		isHidden
		summarizeBy: none
		sourceColumn: merchant_sk

	column attempt_count
		dataType: int64
		formatString: #,0
		summarizeBy: sum
		sourceColumn: attempt_count

	column approved_count
		dataType: int64
		formatString: #,0
		summarizeBy: sum
		sourceColumn: approved_count

	column declined_count
		dataType: int64
		formatString: #,0
		summarizeBy: sum
		sourceColumn: declined_count

	/// **The non-additive column.** `summarizeBy: none` is the first line of defence: dragged onto
	/// a visual directly it produces nothing rather than a sum. The second line is that the only
	/// measure over it refuses to answer above day grain.
	///
	/// One further precision the warehouse forces and the column name hides: the proc computes
	/// `COUNT_BIG(DISTINCT f.account_sk)`, and `account_sk` identifies an SCD2 *version*, not an
	/// account. So this is strictly "distinct account versions that transacted at this merchant
	/// that day". It equals distinct accounts unless an account's version changed during that same
	/// day, in which case it is one too high for that account. That residual is real, is bounded by
	/// the number of same-day SCD2 changes, and is the price of the aggregate being computable with
	/// a GROUP BY. `[Distinct Accounts]` below goes through `dim_account[account_id]` and does not
	/// have it — which is another reason the exact measure is worth keeping alongside the fast one.
	column distinct_account_count
		dataType: int64
		formatString: #,0
		summarizeBy: none
		sourceColumn: distinct_account_count

	column attempted_amount_gbp_minor
		dataType: int64
		formatString: \£#,0.00;(\£#,0.00);\£#,0.00
		summarizeBy: sum
		sourceColumn: attempted_amount_gbp_minor

	column approved_amount_gbp_minor
		dataType: int64
		formatString: \£#,0.00;(\£#,0.00);\£#,0.00
		summarizeBy: sum
		sourceColumn: approved_amount_gbp_minor

	column dispute_count
		dataType: int64
		formatString: #,0
		summarizeBy: sum
		sourceColumn: dispute_count

	column disputed_amount_gbp_minor
		dataType: int64
		formatString: \£#,0.00;(\£#,0.00);\£#,0.00
		summarizeBy: sum
		sourceColumn: disputed_amount_gbp_minor

	column load_batch_id
		dataType: string
		isHidden
		summarizeBy: none
		sourceColumn: load_batch_id

	column loaded_ts
		dataType: dateTime
		isHidden
		formatString: dd/mm/yyyy hh:nn:ss
		summarizeBy: none
		sourceColumn: loaded_ts

	/// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	/// The additive measures — the reason this table exists
	/// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	measure 'Attempts (agg)' = SUM(agg_merchant_daily[attempt_count])
		formatString: #,0
		displayFolder: Merchant daily (agg)

	measure 'Approved (agg)' = SUM(agg_merchant_daily[approved_count])
		formatString: #,0
		displayFolder: Merchant daily (agg)

	measure 'Attempted Volume (agg, GBP)' = SUM(agg_merchant_daily[attempted_amount_gbp_minor])
		formatString: \£#,0.00;(\£#,0.00);\£#,0.00
		displayFolder: Merchant daily (agg)

	/// The same DIVIDE-of-two-SUMs shape as on the detail fact, and it gives the same answer at
	/// every grain — which is the proof that the rule in
	/// `src/warehouse/procs/09_sp_load_agg_merchant_daily.sql` was the right one. Had the proc
	/// stored `approval_rate` per merchant-day, this measure could only have been
	/// `AVERAGE(approval_rate)`, and `tests/test_gold_recon.py` would not have been able to tie it
	/// to the detail fact at any grain above a day.
	measure 'Authorisation Rate (agg)' = DIVIDE([Approved (agg)], [Attempts (agg)])
		formatString: 0.00%
		displayFolder: Merchant daily (agg)

	/// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	/// The non-additive one, fenced
	/// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	/// This is the "marked as day-grain-only" the warehouse proc promises. The marking is not a
	/// comment or a description — it is a guard that makes the wrong number unobtainable:
	/// `HASONEVALUE(dim_date[date_sk])` is true only when the filter context is one day, and the
	/// measure returns BLANK otherwise. A monthly card reads blank rather than reading the sum of
	/// thirty daily distinct counts, which at this data's shape overstates by roughly an order of
	/// magnitude.
	///
	/// `tests/test_gold_recon.py::test_distinct_account_count_is_not_additive_and_is_only_right_per_day`
	/// asserts the summed value strictly exceeds the true overall distinct count, so the hazard
	/// this guard prevents is demonstrated rather than asserted.
	///
	/// Returning BLANK rather than an error string is deliberate: a blank cell in a matrix is
	/// visually obvious and composes with the rest of DAX, whereas a text value would change the
	/// measure's data type and break any visual that expects a number. The cost is that a reader
	/// sees "no value" without being told why — which is what the description below is for, and
	/// why `[Distinct Accounts]` exists as the answer to the question they were actually asking.
	measure 'Distinct Accounts (per day)' = ```
			IF(
			    HASONEVALUE(dim_date[date_sk]),
			    SUM(agg_merchant_daily[distinct_account_count])
			)
			```
		formatString: #,0
		displayFolder: Merchant daily (agg)

	/// The correct answer at any grain, and the reason the guard on
	/// `[Distinct Accounts (per day)]` can afford to be strict.
	/// It goes to the detail fact and counts `dim_account[account_id]` — the **business** key, not
	/// `fact_transaction[account_sk]`, which would count SCD2 versions and double-count any account
	/// whose risk band moved inside the period. That distinction is invisible at day grain and
	/// grows with the window, which makes it exactly the kind of error that survives review.
	///
	/// It is genuinely more expensive than the aggregate — a distinct count across the detail fact
	/// rather than a SUM over one row per merchant-day — and that cost is the honest reason the
	/// aggregate's stored column exists at all. Both are here so the choice is explicit.
	///
	/// `SUMMARIZE` rather than `CALCULATE(DISTINCTCOUNT(dim_account[account_id]), fact_transaction)`.
	/// The CALCULATE form does work, but only via table expansion — passing a fact table as a
	/// filter argument expands it to include the columns of every dimension it points at, which is
	/// a real DAX rule and an obscure one. SUMMARIZE says the same thing directly: scan
	/// fact_transaction in the current filter context, group by the related account business key,
	/// count the groups. The many-to-one traversal from fact to dimension it relies on holds
	/// regardless of `crossFilteringBehavior`, which is why this needs no CROSSFILTER either.
	measure 'Distinct Accounts' = ```
			COUNTROWS(
			    SUMMARIZE(fact_transaction, dim_account[account_id])
			)
			```
		formatString: #,0
		displayFolder: Accounts

	partition agg_merchant_daily = entity
		mode: directLake
		source
			entityName: agg_merchant_daily
			schemaName: dbo
			expressionSource: DatabaseQuery
